# Throughput floors for scripts/protocol-matrix.sh --floors on macOS.
#
# Same format and the same "half the lowest observed ratio" rule as
# scripts/protocol-matrix.floors, calibrated on the developer machine
# (macOS arm64, Apple M2, 2026-11-10) instead of the shared Linux runner.
#
# Why the two files differ: the matrix measures one 64 KiB write followed by a
# 64 KiB read, which is a frame-latency shape, and its rate tracks the ratio of
# chunk to bridge buffer. macOS is ~2x slower in that shape at the shipped
# 64 KiB buffer, so the Linux floors fire on a healthy Mac (measured: ws-plain
# 162.8 MiB/s against a Linux floor of 0.39 x 527.3 = 205.6 MiB/s).
#
# Calibration run (tcp-plain 527.3 MiB/s):
#   tcp-tls 350.2 (0.664)   tcp-tls-mux 309.3 (0.587)   ws-plain 162.8 (0.309)
#   ws-tls 133.6 (0.253)    ws-tls-mux 176.1 (0.334)    wss 137.0 (0.260)
#   kcp-plain 63.6 (0.121)  kcp-tls 66.5 (0.126)        kcp-tls-mux 52.1 (0.099)
#   quic 88.0 (0.167)
#
# The absolute tcp-plain floor is halved from this host's figure (527 / 2 = 263
# -> 250) rather than from a Linux run, for the same reason the ratios are.
#
# This file gates nothing in CI; it exists so `--floors` is meaningful locally.

tcp-plain=250
tcp-tls=ratio:0.33
tcp-tls-mux=ratio:0.29
ws-plain=ratio:0.15
ws-tls=ratio:0.12
ws-tls-mux=ratio:0.16
wss=ratio:0.13
kcp-plain=ratio:0.06
kcp-tls=ratio:0.06
kcp-tls-mux=ratio:0.04
quic=ratio:0.08
